// 200 path requests on the 256 by 256 cell playfield (WP4.2, the load test of
// docs/rules/pathfinding.md). The map is assets/maps/playfield256: a flat 1,024 m
// field with two rivers, rock blocks, ore, a road and a bridge. Two hundred light
// tanks stand on a grid of cells in its north-west corner (x and y 6 to 63, every
// third cell with no rock or water next to it) and all get the order, on tick 1,
// to go to a different cell of the south-east corner (x and y 186 to 251, picked
// the same way). The routes are some 300 cells long and cross the rivers; every
// unit has to be served by the path service, going round the slots from the lowest, inside its budget
// of nodes per tick.
//
// tools/sf_headless/tests/pathing.rs runs it, asserts that the service never
// spends more than its budget in a tick, that the requests (all asked in the
// same tick) are served in the order of the entity ids, and prints how many ticks it took to serve them all.
// CI runs it on Linux and Windows and requires the checksum in
// pathing_256.expected. The unit definitions are the frozen ones of `pathing`.
//
// Slots 0..200 are the tanks, in the order of the cells (rows first); the
// order of slot `i` goes to the goal listed `i`-th below.
Scenario(
    name: "pathing_256",
    seed: 0x7E44_0043,
    map: (width: 256, height: 256),
    map_name: Some("playfield256"),
    unit_defs: Some("pathing_defs"),
    ticks: 600,
    commands: [
        (tick: 0, command: SpawnUnit(kind: 19, owner: 0, cell: (6, 6))), // slot 0
